          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I keep getting urine infections, three this year. Is recurrent infection something to escalate, and what does guidance describe about managing it?",
          "answer": "Recurrent urinary infections should prompt you to seek medical advice, especially if your symptoms are severe or worsening. It's important to discuss the pattern of these infections with a healthcare provider, as they may indicate a need for further evaluation or a tailored management approach.\n\nRecurrent urinary infections are defined as having two or more infections within six months or three or more within a year. Management often includes conducting a urine culture to aid in selecting the appropriate treatment. Most patients typically see improvement within 48 hours of initiating treatment, and if symptoms do not improve, further assessment is warranted rather than merely extending the same treatment (source: guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment).\n\nIn managing these infections, strategies may include a short course of antibiotics, such as nitrofurantoin or trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, while considering local resistance patterns. Preventive measures might be recommended as well, such as increasing fluid intake, urinating after sexual intercourse, and avoiding irritants like spermicides (source: guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment). \n\nThere are differing opinions on management strategies, including some guidance suggesting symptomatic treatment for mild cases, while others recommend immediate antibiotic therapy to prevent complications (source: general clinical reference). Discussing your situation with your clinician will help determine the best approach for your specific case.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
